/* Markov chain babble — order-2 word chain over a small corpus.

   Builds a map from "word word" pairs to the series of words that followed
   them in the corpus, then walks the chain picking random successors.
   Seeded, so a run is reproducible within one backend — but the interpreter
   and a compiled binary use different generators, so they will not agree
   with each other. That is documented, and it is why this example needs
   special handling in the test harness.

   The corpus is written circularly (the tail feeds back into the head) so
   the chain has no dead ends. */

Pull a book on chance.

Bind map from text to series of text to transitions-of, given (the series of text words):
    Define transitions as a map from text to series of text with ().
    Define count as the number of words.
    Define limit as count - 1.
    Define index as 1.
    While index is less than limit, repeat:
        Define word as item index of words.
        Define second-index as index + 1.
        Define second-word as item second-index of words.
        Define third-index as index + 2.
        Define follower as item third-index of words.
        Define bigram as "{word} {second-word}".
        Define fresh as a series of text with ().
        Define bucket as the entry for bigram in transitions but void is fresh.
        Add follower to bucket.
        In transitions, the entry for bigram becomes bucket.
        The index becomes index + 1.
    Done.
    Return transitions.
Done.

Bind text to babble, given (the map from text to series of text transitions,
                            the series of text opening-pair, the number how-many):
    Define prev as the first of opening-pair.
    Define curr as item 2 of opening-pair.
    Define out as "{prev} {curr}".
    Define steps as 1.
    While steps is not greater than how-many, repeat:
        Define bigram as "{prev} {curr}".
        Define fresh as a series of text with ().
        Define nexts as the entry for bigram in transitions but void is fresh.
        If the number of nexts is 0, stop.
        Define pick as a random item from nexts but void is "".
        The out becomes "{out} {pick}".
        The prev becomes curr.
        The curr becomes pick.
        The steps becomes steps + 1.
    Done.
    Return out.
Done.

Bind void to play:
    Seed the chance with 42.

    Define corpus as "in the beginning there was no fire and the earth was cold 
    then the Thunderbirds sent their lightning to a sycamore tree on an island where the Weasels lived 
    the Weasels were the only ones who had fire and they would not give any of it away
    the people knew that there was fire on the island because they could see smoke coming from the sycamore but the water was too deep for anyone to cross
    when winter came the people suffered so much from the cold that they called a council to find some way of obtaining fire from the Weasels
    they invited all the animals who could swim
    how shall we obtain fire the people asked
    most of the animals were afraid of the Weasels because they were bloodthirsty and ate mice and moles and fish and birds
    Rabbit was the only one who was brave enough to try to steal fire from them
    I can run and swim faster than the Weasels he said
    I am also a good dancer 
    every night the Weasels build a big fire and dance around it
    tonight I will swim across and join in the dancing 
    I will run away with some fire
    he considered the matter for a while and then decided how he would do it
    before the sun set he rubbed his head with pine tar so as to make his hair stand up
    then, as darkness was falling he swam across to the island
    the Weasels received Rabbit gladly because they had heard of his fame as a dancer 
    soon they had a big fire blazing and all began dancing around it
    as the Weasels danced they approached nearer and nearer the fire in the centre of the circle
    they would bow to the fire and then dance backwards away from it
    when Rabbit entered the dancing circle the Weasels shouted to him lead us Rabbit
    he danced ahead of them coming closer and closer to the fire
    he bowed to the fire, bringing his head lower and lower as if he were going to take hold of it
    while the Weasels were dancing faster and faster trying to keep up with him Rabbit suddenly bowed very low so that the pine tar in his hair caught fire in a flash of flame
    he ran off with his head ablaze and the angry Weasels pursued him crying 
    catch him catch him He has stolen our sacred fire catch him, and throw him down
    but Rabbit outran them and plunged into the water leaving the Weasels on the shore
    he swam across the water with the flames still blazing from his hair
    the Weasels now called on the Thunderbirds to make it rain so as to extinguish the fire stolen by Rabbit
    for three days rain poured down upon the earth, and the Weasels were sure that no fire was left burning except in their sycamore tree
    Rabbit however had built a fire in a hollow tree and when the rain stopped and the sun shone he came out and gave fire to all the people
    after that whenever it rained they kept fires in their shelters and that is how Rabbit brought fire to the people".

    Define words as corpus split by " ".
    Define transitions as cast transitions-of on (words).
    Define opening as a series of text with ("in", "the").

    State "seed 42, order-2 babble:".
    State cast babble on (transitions, opening, 40).
Done.

Cast play.

Done.